    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d997">

                <pb facs="0933" n="925" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg925"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/16637: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-11-13T16:00:00+02:00">November 13, 1941—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 14—1:04 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>424. The British Ambassador informs me that a friendly diplomatic representative
                    (who had asked that his identity should not be disclosed) had quoted his
                    colleague in Washington as reporting that the Under Secretary had told him
                    President Inönü’s reference to Turkey as the possible “source of peace” (my
                    telegram 406, November 1, 6 p.m.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d997-f1"/>) had created a
                    bad impression in Washington where it was regarded as a warning to the Western
                    Powers that Turkey would not resist an eventual German demand for
                    cooperation.</p>
                <p>If the Under Secretary has been correctly quoted I should point out that my
                    original comment on Ismet’s speech (my No. 409, November 3, <pb facs="0934"
                        n="926" xml:id="pg926"/> 6 p.m.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d997-f2"/>) made no
                    reference to the passage in question since it was not until the development
                    reported in my telegram No. 422, November 11, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d997-f3"/> that there seemed to be occasion to comment upon a
                    passage which I consider quite materialist [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >immaterial?</hi>] and without concrete significance.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d997-f1" n="9" xml:id="d997-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported the speech by the President of Turkey at the opening of
                    the Grand National Assembly (867.00/3115).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d997-f2" n="10" xml:id="d997-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d997-f3" n="11" xml:id="d997-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported German propaganda to the effect that the Turkish
                    President was cautiously suggesting a plan for Turkey to propose in the near
                    future negotiations for a compromise peace (740.0011 European War
                    1939/16518).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
